WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.282
Lesson two point two.

2
00:00:01.637 --> 00:00:03.943
How a web crawl of MIDI files became a

3
00:00:03.943 --> 00:00:05.835
corpus of guitar and drum songs.

4
00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:12.019
Here's a real file from Lakh, one of the

5
00:00:12.019 --> 00:00:13.587
samples we pulled out to study.

6
00:00:13.891 --> 00:00:15.915
Its header says four hundred and eighty

7
00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.685
ticks per beat, and seven tracks.

8
00:00:17.989 --> 00:00:20.367
MIDI counts time in ticks: here a beat is

9
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:22.593
four hundred and eighty of them, so a bar

10
00:00:22.593 --> 00:00:24.516
of four beats is nineteen hundred and

11
00:00:24.516 --> 00:00:24.870
twenty.

12
00:00:25.301 --> 00:00:26.840
A file is split into tracks.

13
00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.642
Track zero holds no notes at all, only the

14
00:00:29.642 --> 00:00:32.115
conductor's information: the tempo, a

15
00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:34.423
hundred and forty beats per minute, and

16
00:00:34.423 --> 00:00:35.522
the time signatures.

17
00:00:35.957 --> 00:00:38.110
Tracks one to six hold the notes, and

18
00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.316
every note names one of sixteen channels.

19
00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:43.062
By convention channel ten is the drums:

20
00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:45.322
there a note number names a drum, not a

21
00:00:45.322 --> 00:00:45.645
pitch.

22
00:00:45.968 --> 00:00:48.283
Code counts from zero, so the code calls

23
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:49.144
it channel nine.

24
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.624
On the other channels a program change

25
00:00:51.624 --> 00:00:53.867
picks the sound: one of a hundred and

26
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:55.850
twenty-eight General MIDI instruments.

27
00:00:56.163 --> 00:00:58.353
Numbers twenty-four to thirty-one are the

28
00:00:58.353 --> 00:01:00.492
guitars, thirty-two to thirty-nine the

29
00:01:00.492 --> 00:01:00.857
basses.

30
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.622
This file has three guitar tracks, a bass,

31
00:01:03.622 --> 00:01:05.552
and a synth lead standing in for the

32
00:01:05.552 --> 00:01:05.917
singer.

33
00:01:06.378 --> 00:01:07.556
Now the notes themselves.

34
00:01:07.838 --> 00:01:09.817
The first instruction in this file, one

35
00:01:09.817 --> 00:01:12.125
point seven one four seconds in: note on,

36
00:01:12.125 --> 00:01:14.103
channel nine, note thirty-six, a kick

37
00:01:14.103 --> 00:01:15.940
drum, at velocity a hundred and four.

38
00:01:16.223 --> 00:01:18.248
Velocity is how hard the note is struck,

39
00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:20.132
from zero to a hundred and twenty-seven.

40
00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:22.346
At the same moment the guitar plays note

41
00:01:22.346 --> 00:01:24.371
forty-three, a low G, at a hundred and

42
00:01:24.371 --> 00:01:24.795
fourteen.

43
00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.565
A note off ends each note.

44
00:01:26.884 --> 00:01:29.115
Our pipeline keeps only the starts, the

45
00:01:29.115 --> 00:01:31.665
onsets: when, which note, and how hard.

46
00:01:31.984 --> 00:01:33.365
Durations are thrown away.

47
00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:39.066
Our code gives every track one of three

48
00:01:39.066 --> 00:01:40.398
roles, and the rule is short.

49
00:01:40.656 --> 00:01:42.547
Drums: anything on channel ten, plus

50
00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:44.309
tracks whose name says they're drums and

51
00:01:44.309 --> 00:01:46.157
whose notes sit, at least ninety percent

52
00:01:46.157 --> 00:01:47.748
of them, on General MIDI drum keys.

53
00:01:48.005 --> 00:01:49.768
Guitar: any channel playing program

54
00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:50.885
twenty-four to thirty-one.

55
00:01:51.143 --> 00:01:52.733
Bass: thirty-two to thirty-nine.

56
00:01:52.991 --> 00:01:54.839
Everything else is dropped, so the synth

57
00:01:54.839 --> 00:01:55.269
lead goes.

58
00:01:56.069 --> 00:01:58.318
The three guitar tracks are merged into

59
00:01:58.318 --> 00:01:59.218
one guitar part.

60
00:01:59.555 --> 00:02:02.029
Fifty-six notes, plus two thousand, two

61
00:02:02.029 --> 00:02:04.278
hundred and seventy-three, plus seven

62
00:02:04.278 --> 00:02:06.920
hundred and forty-two: three thousand and

63
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.382
seventy-one guitar onsets.

64
00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:11.258
The drums have one thousand, four hundred

65
00:02:11.258 --> 00:02:13.259
and seventy-six hits, every one at

66
00:02:13.259 --> 00:02:15.964
velocity a hundred and four: typed in, not

67
00:02:15.964 --> 00:02:16.343
played.

68
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.939
The bass has six hundred and eighty-eight

69
00:02:18.939 --> 00:02:19.263
notes.

70
00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:25.131
Now the filter, run on the twenty-first of

71
00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:26.733
August over the whole archive.

72
00:02:27.054 --> 00:02:29.190
These are the counts for version two of

73
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:29.778
the corpus.

74
00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:32.342
We start with a hundred and seventy-eight

75
00:02:32.342 --> 00:02:34.426
thousand, five hundred and sixty-one

76
00:02:34.426 --> 00:02:34.746
files.

77
00:02:35.303 --> 00:02:37.938
Stage one: does the file parse at all,

78
00:02:37.938 --> 00:02:39.198
with the mido library?

79
00:02:39.542 --> 00:02:41.546
Four thousand and twenty-two don't.

80
00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.353
Lakh's file names are already the MD5 hash

81
00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:46.587
of each file's bytes, so a check for

82
00:02:46.587 --> 00:02:48.420
identical files removes nothing.

83
00:02:48.914 --> 00:02:50.889
Stage two is the de-duplication that

84
00:02:50.889 --> 00:02:51.316
matters.

85
00:02:51.636 --> 00:02:53.664
The same music saved with a different

86
00:02:53.664 --> 00:02:55.959
header has a different hash, so we use a

87
00:02:55.959 --> 00:02:58.254
published list of near-duplicates, found

88
00:02:58.254 --> 00:03:00.496
by two similarity models that both had to

89
00:03:00.496 --> 00:03:02.258
agree above zero point nine nine.

90
00:03:02.578 --> 00:03:04.873
Thirty-seven thousand, eight hundred and

91
00:03:04.873 --> 00:03:05.994
eighty-nine files go.

92
00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.910
Stage three is the big cut: the file must

93
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.968
have a guitar part with at least fifty

94
00:03:10.968 --> 00:03:13.025
notes, and a drum part with at least

95
00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:13.342
fifty.

96
00:03:13.658 --> 00:03:16.191
Over seventy-seven thousand files go here;

97
00:03:16.191 --> 00:03:18.565
most have no declared guitar at all, and a

98
00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.940
three-note guitar part is a mistake, not a

99
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.203
part.

100
00:03:21.661 --> 00:03:24.161
Stage four: at least ninety-five percent

101
00:03:24.161 --> 00:03:26.498
of the file's time must be in four four,

102
00:03:26.498 --> 00:03:28.780
because the grid the models read is built

103
00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:29.758
of four-beat bars.

104
00:03:30.085 --> 00:03:32.422
Six thousand, two hundred and fifty-nine

105
00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:32.585
go.

106
00:03:33.064 --> 00:03:35.136
Stage five: a sane length, eight to a

107
00:03:35.136 --> 00:03:37.069
thousand bars and twenty seconds to forty

108
00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:37.438
minutes.

109
00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:39.694
A tempo map that gives an absurd length is

110
00:03:39.694 --> 00:03:41.029
a corrupt file, not a song.

111
00:03:41.305 --> 00:03:43.101
A hundred and ninety-one go, leaving

112
00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:44.942
fifty-two thousand, eight hundred and

113
00:03:44.942 --> 00:03:46.462
twenty-four songs in version two.

114
00:03:46.738 --> 00:03:48.718
Version three, in chapter six, ends at

115
00:03:48.718 --> 00:03:50.559
fifty-two thousand, seven hundred and

116
00:03:50.559 --> 00:03:51.158
ninety-three.

117
00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.875
Stage three removed the most, so look at

118
00:03:56.875 --> 00:03:57.730
what it removed.

119
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.292
Thirty-three thousand files had neither a

120
00:04:00.292 --> 00:04:02.854
guitar nor drums: files without guitar are

121
00:04:02.854 --> 00:04:05.097
mostly the orchestral, karaoke and easy

122
00:04:05.097 --> 00:04:06.431
listening end of the web.

123
00:04:06.751 --> 00:04:09.100
Eight thousand had a guitar but no drums,

124
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.396
and under two thousand had both, but too

125
00:04:11.396 --> 00:04:11.930
few notes.

126
00:04:12.371 --> 00:04:14.789
And thirty-three thousand, nine hundred

127
00:04:14.789 --> 00:04:17.034
and forty-five files have drums but no

128
00:04:17.034 --> 00:04:17.955
declared guitar.

129
00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.776
A rock song whose guitar was typed in on a

130
00:04:20.776 --> 00:04:23.309
piano or synth sound lands here, and only

131
00:04:23.309 --> 00:04:25.439
listening could tell it apart from a

132
00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:26.245
keyboard song.

133
00:04:26.739 --> 00:04:27.487
One more gap.

134
00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:30.307
A channel that never gets a program change

135
00:04:30.307 --> 00:04:32.897
plays program zero, a piano, so a guitar

136
00:04:32.897 --> 00:04:35.084
part left on the default is invisible.

137
00:04:35.429 --> 00:04:37.789
A probe of four thousand files puts that

138
00:04:37.789 --> 00:04:40.148
at roughly seven hundred and fifty files

139
00:04:40.148 --> 00:04:41.184
across the corpus.

140
00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.451
So the rule's errors lean one way.

141
00:04:43.774 --> 00:04:45.875
It trusts the declared program, so a

142
00:04:45.875 --> 00:04:48.136
mislabelled part can still slip in, but

143
00:04:48.136 --> 00:04:50.398
the big losses are real guitar songs left

144
00:04:50.398 --> 00:04:50.614
out.

145
00:04:50.937 --> 00:04:53.091
With about fifty thousand songs left,

146
00:04:53.091 --> 00:04:55.407
there was enough to keep only what we were

147
00:04:55.407 --> 00:04:56.107
confident in.

148
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:00.590
Back to our file.

149
00:05:00.918 --> 00:05:03.160
Stage four asks what share of the file's

150
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.308
time is in four four.

151
00:05:04.636 --> 00:05:06.824
Count the ticks spent under a four four

152
00:05:06.824 --> 00:05:09.230
signature, and divide by all the ticks in

153
00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:09.722
the file.

154
00:05:10.383 --> 00:05:12.433
Here's the whole file as one strip of

155
00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:12.757
ticks.

156
00:05:13.081 --> 00:05:15.401
It has eight bars in two four, each half

157
00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:16.857
as long as a four four bar.

158
00:05:17.181 --> 00:05:19.231
Together that's seven thousand, six

159
00:05:19.231 --> 00:05:20.580
hundred and eighty ticks.

160
00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:23.520
So a hundred and ninety-two thousand ticks

161
00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.138
of a hundred and ninety-nine thousand, six

162
00:05:26.138 --> 00:05:28.873
hundred and eighty are in four four: zero

163
00:05:28.873 --> 00:05:30.269
point nine six one five.

164
00:05:30.618 --> 00:05:33.120
Above zero point nine five, so it passes.

165
00:05:33.584 --> 00:05:35.877
Why a share of time, with five percent to

166
00:05:35.877 --> 00:05:36.190
spare?

167
00:05:36.503 --> 00:05:38.640
A few short odd bars in a long song cost

168
00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:40.933
little, so the rule keeps songs like this

169
00:05:40.933 --> 00:05:41.142
one.

170
00:05:41.455 --> 00:05:43.800
Four thousand, three hundred and fifty-six

171
00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.989
of the final songs contain some bars that

172
00:05:45.989 --> 00:05:46.875
aren't four four.

173
00:05:47.188 --> 00:05:49.377
Lesson two point three shows how the grid

174
00:05:49.377 --> 00:05:50.211
copes with them.

175
00:05:50.651 --> 00:05:53.238
Stage five counts bars the simple way: all

176
00:05:53.238 --> 00:05:55.502
the ticks, over four hundred and eighty

177
00:05:55.502 --> 00:05:57.119
ticks a beat times four beats.

178
00:05:57.443 --> 00:05:59.868
A hundred and four bars, and a hundred and

179
00:05:59.868 --> 00:06:00.893
eighty-two seconds.

180
00:06:01.216 --> 00:06:03.480
Well inside the limits, so this file is

181
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.097
one of the fifty-two thousand.

182
00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:10.317
One rule had a second version.

183
00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:12.768
Some files write a drum kit onto an

184
00:06:12.768 --> 00:06:15.336
ordinary channel, not channel ten, so a

185
00:06:15.336 --> 00:06:17.786
repair rule rescues tracks that look like

186
00:06:17.786 --> 00:06:18.137
drums.

187
00:06:18.583 --> 00:06:20.352
Version two rescued a track if its name

188
00:06:20.352 --> 00:06:22.120
said drums and nine in ten of its notes

189
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.109
sat on drum keys, or if its pitches looked

190
00:06:24.109 --> 00:06:24.595
like a kit.

191
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.673
An audit on the ninth of September found

192
00:06:26.673 --> 00:06:28.441
the pitch test was letting in piano and

193
00:06:28.441 --> 00:06:30.651
bass parts: of three thousand, six hundred

194
00:06:30.651 --> 00:06:32.287
and sixteen tracks it rescued, one

195
00:06:32.287 --> 00:06:34.232
thousand, nine hundred and thirty-six had

196
00:06:34.232 --> 00:06:36.000
under a fifth of their notes on kick,

197
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:36.928
snare or hat pitches.

198
00:06:37.293 --> 00:06:39.219
Why the pitch-range test failed: a bass

199
00:06:39.219 --> 00:06:40.888
line in E major sits on notes forty,

200
00:06:40.888 --> 00:06:42.686
forty-two, forty-four, forty-nine and

201
00:06:42.686 --> 00:06:44.398
fifty-one, which General MIDI calls a

202
00:06:44.398 --> 00:06:46.239
snare, two hi-hats, a crash and a ride.

203
00:06:46.495 --> 00:06:48.250
A sharper test, the share of a track's

204
00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:50.348
notes on kit pitches, does separate them:

205
00:06:50.348 --> 00:06:51.974
at zero point eight it would win back

206
00:06:51.974 --> 00:06:53.644
about five hundred and fifty-five real

207
00:06:53.644 --> 00:06:53.858
kits.

208
00:06:54.114 --> 00:06:56.041
It wasn't built; the choice was to stay

209
00:06:56.041 --> 00:06:56.426
cautious.

210
00:06:57.321 --> 00:06:59.305
Version three drops the pitch test.

211
00:06:59.645 --> 00:07:01.969
A track is rescued only if its name says

212
00:07:01.969 --> 00:07:04.463
drums, it isn't a General MIDI instrument

213
00:07:04.463 --> 00:07:07.014
like steel drums, nine in ten of its notes

214
00:07:07.014 --> 00:07:09.394
are on drum keys, and at least half are

215
00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:09.961
kit notes.

216
00:07:10.401 --> 00:07:12.267
The result: fifty-two thousand, eight

217
00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:13.802
hundred and twenty-four songs became

218
00:07:13.802 --> 00:07:15.461
fifty-two thousand, seven hundred and

219
00:07:15.461 --> 00:07:16.000
ninety-three.

220
00:07:16.249 --> 00:07:18.073
Two hundred and seventy-two left, and two

221
00:07:18.073 --> 00:07:19.732
hundred and forty-one joined, where a

222
00:07:19.732 --> 00:07:21.266
rescued drum track had really been a

223
00:07:21.266 --> 00:07:21.557
guitar.

224
00:07:21.806 --> 00:07:23.589
Songs with a rescued track fell from three

225
00:07:23.589 --> 00:07:25.372
thousand, six hundred and thirty-four to

226
00:07:25.372 --> 00:07:27.197
two hundred and forty-five, and the store

227
00:07:27.197 --> 00:07:28.773
holds about a million fewer drum hits.

228
00:07:29.111 --> 00:07:29.896
It had a price.

229
00:07:30.211 --> 00:07:32.306
Eight hundred and ninety-five real kits

230
00:07:32.306 --> 00:07:34.192
sat on other channels with blank or

231
00:07:34.192 --> 00:07:36.445
unhelpful names, and version three loses

232
00:07:36.445 --> 00:07:39.012
them: in the songs that stayed, toms fell

233
00:07:39.012 --> 00:07:41.422
by fifty-five percent, crashes forty-six,

234
00:07:41.422 --> 00:07:42.417
rides thirty-eight.

235
00:07:42.731 --> 00:07:43.936
Both versions are kept.

236
00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.136
The drummers train on version three.

237
00:07:47.713 --> 00:07:49.876
Five stages: it parses, it isn't a

238
00:07:49.876 --> 00:07:52.193
duplicate, it has a real guitar part and a

239
00:07:52.193 --> 00:07:54.356
real drum part, it's nearly all in four

240
00:07:54.356 --> 00:07:55.953
four, and it's a sane length.

241
00:07:56.262 --> 00:07:57.601
What's left is the corpus.

242
00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.331
Next, lesson two point three: how those

243
00:08:00.331 --> 00:08:02.494
songs are put on the grid the models read.

